Reviewed product design inputs. tokens.json and components.json are extracted by hand from two design sources and
checked in, so the application does not depend on those sources at build time.

These files are the only design input. Nothing else is layered underneath them; every colour, font, size, radius,
shadow and component dimension in the generated theme comes from here.

Sources (SHA-256 at extraction):
- bitflag-theme.css (shared component styling: buttons, tags, inputs, cards, tables, dialogs): 519bc55f75f69af87279b47e5c50c29259b332dd330dadd4d359009e8d065916
- BitFlag Overview (shell layout and the light and dark colour roles): ca14ebe91bda8b47180cfca57f2429204c8ae511cf70a47ee757133abb3e5dbf

Mapping notes
- Colour roles come from the Overview shell. Component dimensions and radii come from the shared theme.
- Where the sources disagree, the Overview wins for roles and the shared theme wins for component geometry.
- Source colours that failed contrast were darkened in tokens.json, not the threshold relaxed:
  - Light success text #059669 (3.8:1) became #047857 on white. The original stays as the success marker colour.
  - Light warning and danger text use darker steps (#92400e, #b91c1c) so labels stay legible on their tinted backgrounds.
  - The source divider colours (about 1.2:1) are kept for decorative rules only. Form control boundaries use a separate
    control-border role at 3:1 or more.
- The source "faint" text colour (2.5:1) is dropped; muted is the lowest-emphasis text role.
- Source rgba overlays (borders, hover, soft fills) are resolved to concrete colours so contrast can be verified.
- The shared theme has no focus-visible background; the 2px accent outline with 2px offset is kept.

Fonts: Inter and Azeret Mono are served from the application through @fontsource-variable/inter and
@fontsource-variable/azeret-mono (both SIL Open Font License 1.1). The npm lockfile pins the font files.
